{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, the sun was setting.</b> As in ״וַיְהִי הֵם מְרִיקִים שַׂקֵּיהֶם״ (Genesis 42), ״וַיְהִי הֵם קֹבְרִים אִישׁ״ (II Kings 13) — that is to say, 'and this thing came to pass:'" "<b>The sun was setting.</b> It had set." "<b>And a thick darkness.</b> The darkness of day." "<b>And behold, a smoking furnace, etc.</b> He intimated to him that the kingdoms would fall into Gehinnom." "<b>בָּאָה.</b> Its accent is above; therefore it is explained that it had already come. And if its accent were below — בָּאָלֶ\"ף — it would be explained as 'while it was setting.' And one cannot say thus, for it is already written ״וַיְהִי הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ לָבוֹא״, and the passing of the smoking furnace took place after that — so it follows that it had already set. And this distinction applies to every feminine word whose base is two letters, such as בָּא, קָם, שָׁב: when the accent is above, it is past tense — like this instance, and like ״וְרָחֵל בָּאָה״, ״קָמָה אֲלֻמָּתִי״, ״הִנֵּה שָׁבָה יְבִמְתֵּךְ״; and when the accent is below, it is present tense — a thing that is happening now and ongoing — as in ״בָּאָה עִם הַצֹּאן״, ״בָּעֶרֶב הִיא בָּאָה וּבַבֹּקֶר הִיא שָׁבָה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {4 "Rashi writes בָּאָלֶ\"ף — literally 'with alef' — to indicate the word בָּאָה read with the accent on the final syllable (milra), which shifts the prosodic grouping; kept as written in Hebrew with the abbreviation mark."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:56.206486914Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}